# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# tracks — onboarded 2026-10-17 at James's tracks#1 (the repo was music-miles)
#
#   "Can you onboard these projects onto kit even if you don't start working
#   on them yet" … "I am kind of thinking just call it "Tracks" with a tag line
#   of track your tracks tracks"
#
# The repo was empty at onboarding, so there was no code to infer from. Everything here comes
# from what he has written on tracks#1: a running app that puts the music
# you listened to onto the route you ran. He renamed the repo to tracks on 2026-10-17, and
# the corpus follows it.
#
#   DEFINED   — his words on tracks#1. Cites the issue.
#   INFERRED  — my reading of the idea, or a detail it needs that he hasn't
#               stated. Each is `review unreviewed`: it shows in Proposed, and
#               he can deny it. Some lean on the Gemini reply he pasted there,
#               which he has not endorsed line by line.
#
# Nothing is built. The first slice is tracks#2 (scaffold) and tracks#3 (BEH-MAP-1/2/3).
#
# 2026-10-17, his steer on tracks#3:
#
#   "Let's not worry about the track visuals for now the main focus is we need
#   to be able to identify when the user has finished a Strava run and identify
#   which songs they have been listening to … This will require Strava and
#   Spotify integration … This will probably create kit backend specs"
#
# So FEAT-SYNC below is the priority, and the visual behaviours (BEH-MAP-1,
# BEH-SHARE-1) drop to priority 3 until he says otherwise.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

feature FEAT-BRAND "Name and voice"
  stage ui
feature FEAT-SYNC "Noticing a finished run and the songs played during it"
  stage ui
feature FEAT-CONNECT "Connecting Strava and Spotify"
  stage ui
# FEAT-MAP and FEAT-SHARE read as built until 2026-10-26: their cards predate `pending`. The map's
# UI is drafted in tracks#3 on demo data, unmerged; nothing of sharing exists yet.
feature FEAT-MAP "The music map of a workout"
  stage ux
feature FEAT-SHARE "Sharing a workout"
  stage problem
# His questions of 2026-10-24 14:01-14:03Z, "trying to make posts more interesting".
feature FEAT-HIGHLIGHTS "Highlights of a run, for a more interesting post"
  stage development


# ═══ DEFINED — tracks#1 ═══

behaviour BEH-BRAND-1 "The app is called Tracks, and its tagline is: Track your tracks' tracks"
  in FEAT-BRAND
  layer ux
  source defined tracks#1
  review unreviewed
  priority 1
  actor runner
  when opens page:Home
  then sees region:Hero
  contract "just call it "Tracks" with a tag line of track your tracks tracks": a running track, a GPS track and a music track
  contract unreviewed because he wrote "kind of thinking"; the name and the repo rename stay his


# ═══ DEFINED — tracks#3 (his steer) ═══

behaviour BEH-SYNC-1 "Tracks notices when a runner has finished a Strava run"
  in FEAT-SYNC
  layer technical
  source defined tracks#3
  review unreviewed
  priority 1
  actor runner
  contract "identify when the user has finished a Strava run": Strava pushes a webhook event when an activity is created, so Tracks listens rather than polls
  contract the subscription handshake echoes hub.challenge only when hub.verify_token matches the configured token; any other token is refused
  contract Tracks creates its own push subscription once it is serving, when Strava has none, and never replaces one that points at another callback; GET /api/status reports the outcome as stravaWebhook. Unreviewed: self-registering, and showing it publicly, are my choices
  contract an event counts only when it is an activity create for a connected athlete; updates, deletes and athlete events are acknowledged and ignored
  contract the event is acknowledged at once and processed after, because Strava expects a reply within 2 seconds and retries otherwise
  contract a retried or duplicated event for the same activity is processed once
  contract only runs count (sport types Run, TrailRun, VirtualRun); a ride or a swim is ignored. Unreviewed: the list is my guess

behaviour BEH-SYNC-2 "Tracks identifies the songs the runner listened to during that run"
  in FEAT-SYNC
  layer technical
  source defined tracks#3
  review unreviewed
  priority 1
  actor runner
  contract "identify which songs they have been listening to": read the runner's Spotify recently-played history for the run's time window
  contract a song counts when its play overlaps the window from the run's start to its start plus elapsed time
  contract Spotify's played_at is taken as the moment the play ended, so a play spans played_at minus the track's duration up to played_at. Unreviewed: unverified against real data, so recheck it once his account is connected
  contract recently-played returns at most the last 50 plays, so the history is read as soon as the run is noticed, not later
  contract songs are returned in the order they played; a run with no songs in its window gives an empty list, not an error

behaviour BEH-SYNC-3 "Each finished run is kept with its songs, so the latest one can be fetched"
  in FEAT-SYNC
  layer technical
  source inferred tracks#3
  review approved
  priority 1
  actor runner
  contract GET /api/runs lists the runs Tracks has noticed, newest first, each with the songs from BEH-SYNC-2
  contract runs are kept in the database per runner (BEH-ACCOUNT-5), so a restart forgets nothing

behaviour BEH-SYNC-4 "The home page lists each noticed run with the songs played during it"
  in FEAT-SYNC
  layer ux
  source inferred tracks#3
  review approved
  priority 1
  actor runner
  when opens page:Home
  then sees region:Runs
  contract a plain list, newest run first: its name, date and elapsed time (GET /api/runs carries no distance yet), then its songs in the order they played (title and artist)
  contract no map and no cover-art visuals, per "let's not worry about the track visuals for now" on tracks#3
  contract with no runs yet, it says so and explains a run appears after the next one finishes on Strava
  contract unreviewed because the layout and the fields shown are my guess


# ═══ INFERRED — what the idea needs ═══

behaviour BEH-CONNECT-1 "A runner signs in to Tracks with their Strava account"
  in FEAT-CONNECT
  layer ux
  source inferred tracks#1
  review approved
  priority 1
  actor runner
  when opens page:Home
  when activates button:SignInWithStrava
  then sees region:Runs
  contract signed out, the home page shows the hero and one action, Sign in with Strava, with a note that Tracks only reads Strava and Spotify
  contract the button goes to Strava and returns to the home page signed in. Signing in is how Strava gets connected (BEH-ACCOUNT-1), so there is no separate Connect page
  contract read-only: Tracks never writes to Strava unless BEH-SHARE-2 is approved
  contract unreviewed because the single-page flow is my choice; it replaced the Connect page this spec first named

behaviour BEH-CONNECT-2 "A signed-in runner connects Spotify so their runs get songs"
  in FEAT-CONNECT
  layer ux
  source inferred tracks#1
  review approved
  priority 1
  actor runner
  given runner:SignedInWithoutSpotify
  when opens page:Home
  when activates button:ConnectSpotify
  then sees region:Runs
  contract until Spotify is connected, the home page shows Connect Spotify and says songs cannot be matched without it
  contract once connected, that button goes; Disconnect Spotify and Sign out sit in region:Account below the runs
  contract a run that finishes before Spotify is connected keeps no songs, and none can be added later, because recently-played cannot be read back (BEH-SYNC-2)
  contract read-only: Tracks reads listening history and never controls playback

behaviour BEH-SYNC-5 "After a run, the runner opens Tracks and finds it there with its songs"
  in FEAT-SYNC
  layer ux
  source defined oke-fleet#28
  review unreviewed
  priority 1
  actor runner
  given runner:SignedInWithSpotify
  when opens page:Home
  then sees region:Runs
  contract "It uses Strava hook and then pulls Spotify tracks then records everything in the db for the next time that I go and see it"
  contract there is nothing to press: the run arrives by the Strava webhook (BEH-SYNC-1), its songs are read at once (BEH-SYNC-2) and kept (BEH-SYNC-3), so it is waiting on the next visit
  contract only runs that finish after both accounts are connected appear; older runs are not backfilled
  contract unreviewed because "no backfill" follows from Spotify keeping only the last 50 plays, not from anything he said


# ═══ DEFINED — tracks#6 (multi-user) ═══

# Was BEH-CONNECT-3, "Anyone can sign in and connect their own accounts; each runner
# sees only their own runs" (tracks#6: "We should have multi user sign in I want this
# to be used by other people"). Split into this feature when he approved the split on
# 2026-10-22 11:23Z (BEH-EPIC-9). Its contracts moved into the five children, unchanged.
feature FEAT-ACCOUNTS "Each runner has their own account"
  in FEAT-CONNECT
  stage ui

behaviour BEH-ACCOUNT-1 "Signing in with Strava is the account, held in an HttpOnly session cookie"
  in FEAT-ACCOUNTS
  layer technical
  source inferred tracks#6
  review unreviewed
  priority 1
  actor runner
  contract signing in with Strava is the account: the Strava athlete is the user, so there is no password. Unreviewed: my choice
  contract the session is an HttpOnly cookie, the same mechanism as earnyourbeers. Unreviewed: my choice
  contract Spotify allows 5 users and Strava 10 before a provider review, so this is a friends-and-family ceiling until he asks for more

behaviour BEH-ACCOUNT-2 "An OAuth round-trip that does not bring its state back is refused"
  in FEAT-ACCOUNTS
  layer technical
  source inferred tracks#6
  review unreviewed
  priority 1
  actor runner
  contract each OAuth round-trip carries a random state that must come back unchanged, or the sign-in is refused

behaviour BEH-ACCOUNT-3 "Each runner's tokens live in the database, not in config"
  in FEAT-ACCOUNTS
  layer technical
  source inferred tracks#6
  review unreviewed
  priority 1
  actor runner
  contract each runner's Strava and Spotify refresh tokens live in the database, not in config; config holds only the app's client id and secret per provider and the webhook verify token

behaviour BEH-ACCOUNT-4 "A Strava event reaches only its owner, and a runner who revokes Tracks is deleted"
  in FEAT-ACCOUNTS
  layer technical
  source inferred tracks#6
  review unreviewed
  priority 1
  actor runner
  contract a Strava webhook event is routed by its owner_id to that runner; an athlete who never signed in is acknowledged and ignored
  contract a runner who revokes Tracks on Strava is deleted with their runs. Unreviewed: delete rather than keep is my choice

behaviour BEH-ACCOUNT-5 "A runner sees only their own runs"
  in FEAT-ACCOUNTS
  layer technical
  source inferred tracks#6
  review unreviewed
  priority 1
  actor runner
  contract GET /api/runs needs a signed-in runner and returns only their runs

behaviour BEH-CONNECT-4 "When Strava is full, a new runner is told Tracks is full, not shown Strava's error"
  in FEAT-CONNECT
  layer ux
  source defined tracks.notes.md#2026-10-22-1124
  review approved
  priority 3
  actor runner
  when opens page:Home
  then sees region:Runs
  contract built in tracks#19 (merged to tracks dev 2026-10-22): a 403 naming the cap redirects to /?error=full, and Home shows "Tracks is full for now. Please try again later."; any other 403 is still a 502
  contract "We should probably limit sign ups because of the strava api limits or will strava limit this"
  contract my answer: Strava limits it. It counts connected athletes per app: 1 for a new app, up to 10 once raised in his API settings, and more only after Strava reviews the app. The next athlete's sign-in is refused with 403 "Limit of connected athletes exceeded"
  contract my proposal: no cap in Tracks. A second count that agrees with Strava's is a number to keep in step, and it can only refuse people Strava would let in
  contract my proposal: when Strava refuses, Tracks returns to the home page with "Tracks is full for now", not a raw error. An existing runner signing in again is unaffected, because they are already connected
  contract Spotify is the tighter gate, at 5 users before its own review (BEH-ACCOUNT-1), and nothing in Tracks words that refusal yet either

behaviour BEH-MAP-1 "Each workout shows the songs played during it, pinned along its route"
  in FEAT-MAP
  layer ux
  source inferred tracks#1
  review unreviewed
  pending
  priority 3
  actor runner
  given workout:WithSongs
  when opens page:Workout
  then sees region:RouteMap
  then sees region:SongList
  contract each song sits on the map at the point where it started, shown by its cover art
  contract the list below the map shows the same songs in order, with the distance each one covered

behaviour BEH-MAP-2 "A song is placed on the route by matching when it played to where the runner was"
  in FEAT-MAP
  layer technical
  source inferred tracks#1
  review approved
  pending
  priority 1
  actor runner
  contract a song belongs to a workout when it started between the workout's start and end
  contract its pin is the route point whose timestamp is nearest the song's start
  contract a workout with no songs in its window still shows its route, with no pins

behaviour BEH-MAP-3 "A workout recorded without GPS lists its songs but draws no map"
  in FEAT-MAP
  layer ux
  source inferred tracks#1
  review unreviewed
  pending
  priority 2
  actor runner
  given workout:Indoor
  when opens page:Workout
  then sees region:SongList
  contract treadmill and indoor workouts have time but no route, so songs are listed by elapsed time

behaviour BEH-SHARE-1 "A workout can be saved as a share card: the route with the song covers on it"
  in FEAT-SHARE
  layer ux
  source inferred tracks#1
  review unreviewed
  pending
  priority 3
  actor runner
  given workout:WithSongs
  when opens page:Workout
  when activates button:Share
  then sees region:ShareCard
  contract sized for an Instagram or Strava story, from the Gemini reply he pasted
  contract minimal and high-contrast: the word TRACKS, the route line, the covers

behaviour BEH-SHARE-2 "The songs can be written into the Strava activity's description"
  in FEAT-SHARE
  layer technical
  source inferred tracks#1
  review unreviewed
  pending
  priority 3
  actor runner
  contract only when the runner turns it on: it is the one write Tracks would make to Strava

# ═══ HIGHLIGHTS — his questions in Kit, 2026-10-24 ═══
# Each answers "is Tracks able to…" with yes or no and how, as a proposal he can deny.

behaviour BEH-HIGH-1 "Each song shows the minute of the run it started at"
  in FEAT-HIGHLIGHTS
  layer ux
  source defined tracks.notes.md#2026-10-24-1401
  review unreviewed
  priority 3
  actor runner
  given workout:WithSongs
  when opens page:Workout
  then sees region:SongList
  contract "Is kit able to mark the time in the run that the user started this track"
  contract yes: a song's start is already worked out to place it on the route (BEH-SYNC-2, BEH-MAP-2), so the minute into the run is the same number shown as text
  contract my assumption: shown as elapsed time from the run's start, e.g. 12:40, next to each song, and on the share card
  contract my assumption: a song already playing when the run started reads 0:00
  contract built in tracks#21 (merged to tracks dev 2026-10-26): each song in the runs list leads with its minute into the run; there is no share card yet, so that half waits for one

behaviour BEH-HIGH-2 "A run says whether it was a playlist or an album, and names it"
  in FEAT-HIGHLIGHTS
  layer technical
  source defined tracks.notes.md#2026-10-24-1401
  review unreviewed
  pending
  priority 3
  actor runner
  contract "Is tracks able to identify whether the user was listening to a playlist or an album over the course of their run"
  contract yes: each play in Spotify's recently-played history carries the context it was played from (a playlist, an album or an artist), or none, e.g. a queue
  contract my assumption: when most of the run's songs share one context, the run names it ("Run to: Album name"); otherwise it says the songs were mixed
  contract naming a private playlist needs Spotify's playlist-read-private permission, so a runner who connected before this asks again once

behaviour BEH-HIGH-3 "The song playing at the run's top speed is named"
  in FEAT-HIGHLIGHTS
  layer technical
  source defined tracks.notes.md#2026-10-24-1402
  review unreviewed
  pending
  priority 3
  actor runner
  contract "Is tracks able to identify the track that the user reached their top speed"
  contract yes, for a run with GPS: Strava's activity streams give speed second by second, so the top speed's moment falls inside one song's span
  contract a run with no GPS has no speed stream, so it names no song (as BEH-MAP-3 draws no map)
  contract my assumption: the speed is smoothed over a few seconds first, so one GPS jump does not pick the song

behaviour BEH-HIGH-4 "The song with the fastest average pace is named"
  in FEAT-HIGHLIGHTS
  layer technical
  source defined tracks.notes.md#2026-10-24-1402
  review unreviewed
  pending
  priority 3
  actor runner
  contract "Is tracks able to identify the track with the fastest average pace during the run"
  contract yes, for a run with GPS: the distance covered during each song's span, divided by that span, from the same Strava streams as BEH-HIGH-3
  contract my assumption: a song that overlaps the run by under a minute is not counted, so a skipped track never wins

behaviour BEH-HIGH-5 "Each song shows its BPM"
  in FEAT-HIGHLIGHTS
  layer technical
  source defined tracks.notes.md#2026-10-24-1403
  review unreviewed
  pending
  priority 3
  actor runner
  contract "Is tracks able to identify bpm of a song played during a run"
  contract not from Spotify: its audio-features endpoint, which gave tempo, has refused apps created after 27 November 2024, which includes Tracks's
  contract it would need a second source looked up by song and artist, such as GetSongBPM's free API, which asks for a visible link back
  needs-context "Spotify no longer gives new apps a song's BPM. A free outside source (GetSongBPM) could, in return for a link to them on the page. Use it? If no answer by 2026-11-07, I leave BPM out and keep the other highlights."
